Their vulnerability databases
-----------------------------

Trivy and Grype each download their own vulnerability database, straight from their publishers, on
the host that runs Canyon's executor: Trivy from mirror.gcr.io/aquasec/trivy-db and
ghcr.io/aquasecurity/trivy-db (and its index of Java artifacts, trivy-java-db, from the same
places), or from a mirror the operator names; Grype from https://grype.anchore.io/databases. Canyon
does not ship or redistribute these databases or their contents. Each database's content stays
under its sources' terms, which include:

  - the National Vulnerability Database (NVD), from the U.S. National Institute of Standards and
    Technology. NVD data reaches Canyon only through these databases, and Canyon is not endorsed or
    certified by the NVD.
  - the GitHub Advisory Database, under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International licence
    (CC-BY-4.0): https://github.com/github/advisory-database

Trivy's other sources, as its documentation lists them
(https://trivy.dev/latest/docs/scanner/vulnerability/#data-sources): the security advisories of
Alpine Linux (secdb), Amazon Linux (ALAS), AlmaLinux, Arch Linux, Azure Linux (CBL-Mariner),
Chainguard, Debian (Security Bug Tracker, OVAL), Echo, MinimOS, openSUSE and SUSE (CVRF), Oracle
Linux (OVAL), Photon OS, Red Hat (OVAL, Security Data), Rocky Linux, Ubuntu (CVE Tracker) and Wolfi;
Root.io's patch feed and Seal Security's vulnerability feed; the PHP Security Advisories Database,
the Ruby Advisory Database, the Node.js Ecosystem Security Working Group, the Go Vulnerability
Database, Open Source Vulnerabilities (OSV) for crates.io, the GitLab Advisory Database (Community)
and the Kubernetes official CVE feed.

Grype's other sources, as its documentation lists them
(https://oss.anchore.com/docs/reference/grype/data-sources/): the AlmaLinux OSV Database, Alpine
SecDB, Amazon Linux Security Center, Microsoft Azure Linux and CBL-Mariner OVAL, the Bitnami
Vulnerability Database, Chainguard Security and Chainguard Libraries, the Debian Security Tracker,
ECHO Security, MINIMOS Security, Oracle Linux Security, the Red Hat Security Data API, SUSE Security
OVAL, the Ubuntu CVE Tracker and Wolfi Security; and, for scoring, FIRST's Exploit Prediction
Scoring System (EPSS) and CISA's Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog (KEV).
